## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-region consistency, conflict resolution, and practical MongoDB usage.

## Key Concepts
- CAP trade-offs in distributed systems
- Per-field versioning and conflict resolution
- ReadConcern/WriteConcern in MongoDB

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergePrefs(existing, incoming) {
  const out = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(existing || {}));
  for (const key of Object.keys(incoming)) {
    const inc = incoming[key];
    const cur = out[key];
    const tsInc = inc.__version?.ts ?? 0;
    const tsCur = cur?.__version?.ts ?? -1;
    if (tsInc > tsCur || (tsInc === tsCur && (inc.__version?.clientId ?? 0) < (cur?.__version?.clientId ?? 0))) {
      out[key] = inc;
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this merge under simultaneous updates?
- How would you adapt for field-level strong consistency needs?

## Why This Is Asked
The tokenized explanation approach isolates training data exposure, supporting privacy and auditability for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- JWT-based explanation tokens, HS256 signing
- model_versioning, token rotation, expiry
- input validation and per-user audit

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issue explain token (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function issueExplainToken(user_id, model_version, expiryMs, secret) {
  const payload = { sub: user_id, mv: model_version, exp: Math.floor(Date.now()/1000) + expiryMs/1000 };
  return jwt.sign(payload, secret, { algorithm: 'HS256', audience: user_id });
}
```

